WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.013 --> 00:00:02.680
(offbeat music)

00:00:06.275 --> 00:00:08.330
- [Narrator] With just one blog post,

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.260
a young engineer would go on to completely

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.650
shift diversity hiring in tech.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:15.134
Tracy Chou is not your
average woman in STEM.

00:00:15.134 --> 00:00:17.801
(offbeat music)

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.500
Tracy is a sophomore engineer who has

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:31.810
been employed at some of the top

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.710
tech companies in the world.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.780
- I grew up in Silicon
Valley, the daughter

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.220
of two software engineers
and computer scientists.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.050
So, you could say in some ways

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.540
that it was always in my blood.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:42.410
- [Narrator] But as she entered

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:44.430
into the offices of silicon valley,

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.740
she experienced the unsettling feeling

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.980
that can come from being the lone woman.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:51.210
I had a lot of people flirting with me

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.890
or hitting on me or giving me gifts.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:56.640
It was more than a year
into working full-time

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.970
that I first had the realization

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:01.840
that I might be treated
differently because

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.030
I was a woman.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.570
When I was treated differently,

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:06.430
I thought that it was more due

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.380
to something wrong with me.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.600
As more people started talking

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.500
about these issues, I started to see

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.470
that my experience was not unique

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:15.913
and that I wasn't crazy.

00:01:20.372 --> 00:01:21.770
- [Narrator] There was no question

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.870
in Tracy's mind that
there was a pervasive,

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.200
sexist culture throughout her industry.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.600
But she needed the numbers to prove it.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.910
- There were no numbers
across industry overall,

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.540
and so in the back of my head, I

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.130
would actually keep a running count

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:36.220
of who the other female engineers were

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:37.870
and who the other minorities were.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:41.000
I realized also that people
weren't tracking that data.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:42.630
I think in some ways it was because it's easier

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.110
to ignore the problem if you're not

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.180
looking at any data on it.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.730
That made me think, we really do need

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.430
to put these numbers down.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.560
- [Narrator] So she took
matters into her own hands.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:53.380
She published her company's gender

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.440
diversity stats on Medium.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.240
- More and more people started sharing it.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.930
The first big company that released

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:00.480
their data was Google.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.280
And then all the other companies really

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.750
wanted to follow suit.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.290
Later, seeing Apple and Amazon,

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:07.490
I realized that there was actually

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:10.430
a connection, and that me on my side,

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.120
writing a little Medium post and tweeting

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.620
about it in my little
world actually could have

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.865
an impact on the larger industry.

00:02:16.865 --> 00:02:18.940
(offbeat music)

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.350
- [Narrator] Tracy no longer refers

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.230
to herself as just an engineer.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.030
She's also become one of
tech's most impassioned

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:28.340
diversity advocates,
co-founding Project Include,

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.650
a nonprofit that uses data to help

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.010
tech companies diversify their staff.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:34.800
- Now that we're having
much more discussion

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.280
around diversity, people who are having

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.480
less great experiences and understand

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.090
that it's not necessarily them,

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:43.370
and that there are bigger
structural problems

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.290
that need to be addressed.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.590
Sometimes when I feel down, I'll actually

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.690
be lucky to get someone reaching out,

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:52.670
and sometimes it's a younger woman

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:53.852
in industry, or maybe someone still

00:02:53.852 --> 00:02:56.900
in school who will say, thank
you for doing this work.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.540
I'm doing my little part
to make the industry better

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.561
for the people who are coming after me.

00:03:01.561 --> 00:03:05.061
(inspiring offbeat music)

